{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-89596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ for $s>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-89596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+L{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-89596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ca280d469a247bc207a17f239b2407190cc4fae680de6eb4fb807263351d973"} {"question" "q-89596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-89596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2s}{({s}^{2}+4)({s}^{2}+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+2}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{s+2}{({s}^{2}+4)({s}^{2}+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{2({s}^{2}+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-89596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{3t}-{e}^{-t}$ for $s>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{-t}} & =L{{e}^{3t}}+L{-{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+1},\\,s>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s+1)-(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1-s+3}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ca280d469a247bc207a17f239b2407190cc4fae680de6eb4fb807263351d973"} {"question" "q-331457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2s}{(s-3)(s+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{(s-1)(s+3)}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{s+1}{(s-3)(s+1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} & =L{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}+L{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} \\\\  & =-3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}+6L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =-3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}+6L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}+6\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+25}+\\frac{30}{{s}^{2}+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3s+30}{{s}^{2}+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ca280d469a247bc207a17f239b2407190cc4fae680de6eb4fb807263351d973"} {"question" "q-331505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-331505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{30-3s}{s^2+25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)=\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{s+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+3)+(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ca280d469a247bc207a17f239b2407190cc4fae680de6eb4fb807263351d973"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}-9}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}-9}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "s>-3"] ["math" "s>0"] ["math" "s>3"] ["math" "s\\ge -3"] ["math" "s\\ge 0"] ["math" "s\\ge 3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-340655" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "s>3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-341137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-341137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+5\\text{i})+(s-5\\text{i})}{(s-5\\text{i})(s+5\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-341137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ca280d469a247bc207a17f239b2407190cc4fae680de6eb4fb807263351d973"} {"question" "q-341137" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-341137" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{5s}{{s}^{2}+25}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-25}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-341137" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c420f91b0e9d5595092eef7afba5a456f6bdf"} {"question" "q-341137" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059732/state.json" "file_sha256" "721631f1255d7d7b1e720287e26c